{{!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     services/consent — a minimal consent gate, honest about its limits.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Ghost has no consent API. Nothing platform-side records a choice,
     nothing blocks a script, and {{ghost_head}} will keep emitting whatever
     Ghost itself needs regardless of what a reader clicks here. So this is
     not a consent-management platform: no per-vendor toggles, no consent
     receipts, no geo-targeting, no scanning for scripts it does not know
     about. What it CAN do — and does — is guarantee that the two analytics
     scripts THIS THEME adds do not load, request, or store anything until
     the reader says yes, and that "no" is remembered.

     THE CONTRACT. This file defines window.NSConsent before ga4.hbs and
     posthog.hbs run (analytics.hbs owns that ordering). The gate has three
     states: "granted", "denied", or null (never asked / not yet answered).
     A service partial asks the gate rather than the banner:

         no NSConsent on the page  →  no gate configured; load normally
         state "granted"           →  load now
         state null                →  register via whenGranted(), which
                                      fires if the reader later accepts
         state "denied"            →  do nothing, ask nothing, load nothing

     Null must NOT mean "load and apologise later" — a tracker that runs
     before the answer makes the question a decoration, which is the failure
     mode of most consent banners on the web.

     STORAGE is localStorage under "ns-consent" — the same naming as the
     shared "ns-theme" key, but NOT shared with the LMS: consent given to
     this site's scripts says nothing about another product's. Withdrawal is
     the honest gap: there is no settings surface to reopen this banner, so
     a /privacy page should say that clearing site data resets the choice.
     A real CMP fixes that the day one is warranted; do not grow one here.

     THE CHASSIS is .ns-toast-region + .ns-alert, and the pairing is argued
     rather than accidental. By NSDS's own taxonomy this message has BANNER
     lifetime — true for the whole session until something changes — but
     .ns-banner is in-flow chrome at the top of the page, and a partial
     rendered at the end of <body> cannot claim that slot without landing
     below the footer. The toast REGION supplies what is actually needed:
     fixed bottom-corner placement, --z-toast, and the full-width phone
     fallback. Inside it, .ns-alert supplies the anatomy a toast lacks —
     title, text, actions — because this is a question, not a "saved"
     confirmation, and NSDS's warning about toasts (transient, gone before
     the keyboard reaches them) does not apply: this one persists until
     answered and is dismissed by answering. pointer-events-auto is
     required: the region disables pointer events so stacked toasts do not
     block the page, and .ns-toast is what normally restores them; the alert
     must restore them itself or its buttons are unclickable.

     The region ships hidden. The script reveals it only when no decision is
     stored — and with JavaScript off it stays hidden, which is correct,
     because without JavaScript no tracker can run and there is nothing to
     ask about.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
{{#if @custom.require_analytics_consent}}
<div class="ns-toast-region" id="ns-consent" hidden>
    <div class="ns-alert ns-alert--info pointer-events-auto" role="region"
         aria-label="{{t "Privacy choices"}}">
        <div class="ns-alert__body">
            <p class="ns-alert__title">{{t "Analytics on this site"}}</p>
            <p class="ns-alert__text">
                {{t "Allow anonymous usage analytics? Nothing loads until you choose, and declining is remembered."}}
            </p>
            <div class="ns-alert__actions">
                <button class="ns-btn ns-btn--sm ns-btn--primary" type="button"
                        data-consent="granted">{{t "Allow"}}</button>
                <button class="ns-btn ns-btn--sm ns-btn--ghost" type="button"
                        data-consent="denied">{{t "Decline"}}</button>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<script>
(function () {
    "use strict";
    var KEY = "ns-consent";
    var stored = null;
    try { stored = localStorage.getItem(KEY); } catch (e) { }
    if (stored !== "granted" && stored !== "denied") { stored = null; }

    var region = document.getElementById("ns-consent");
    var waiting = [];

    window.NSConsent = {
        state: stored,
        whenGranted: function (fn) {
            if (this.state === "granted") { fn(); }
            else if (this.state === null) { waiting.push(fn); }
        }
    };

    function decide(value) {
        window.NSConsent.state = value;
        try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, value); } catch (e) { }
        region.hidden = true;
        if (value === "granted") {
            for (var i = 0; i < waiting.length; i += 1) { waiting[i](); }
        }
        waiting.length = 0;
    }

    if (stored === null) {
        region.hidden = false;
        var buttons = region.querySelectorAll("[data-consent]");
        for (var i = 0; i < buttons.length; i += 1) {
            buttons[i].addEventListener("click", function (ev) {
                decide(ev.currentTarget.getAttribute("data-consent"));
            });
        }
    }
})();
</script>
{{/if}}
